{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049008 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100875 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100696 :question/math-academy-id "e-4408" :question/problem "Calculate the product $I_3 B$ given that $I_3$ is the $3 \\times 3$ identity matrix and\n\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "Any matrix, when multiplied by an identity matrix, remains unchanged (assuming that the multiplication is well defined).\n\nThe matrix $I_3$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix and $B$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix too. \n\nTherefore, the multiplication is well defined and \n\n\n$$\nI_3\\cdot B=B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101141 :question/math-academy-id "q-13762" :question/problem "Calculate the product ${I}_{2}B$ given that ${I}_{2}$ is the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix and\n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Any matrix, when multiplied by an identity matrix, remains unchanged (assuming that the multiplication is well defined).\n\nThe matrix ${I}_{2}$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix too. \n\nTherefore, the multiplication is well defined and \n\n$$\n{I}_{2}\\cdot B=B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101142 :question/math-academy-id "q-13763" :question/problem "Calculate the product $AI_{3}$ given that $I_{3}$ is the $3 \\cdot 3$ identity matrix and\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 8 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265862 :question/math-academy-id "q-130243" :question/problem "Calculate the product $A{I}_{2}$ given that ${I}_{2}$ is the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix and\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Any matrix, when multiplied by an identity matrix, remains unchanged (assuming that the multiplication is well defined).\n\nThe matrix $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and ${I}_{2}$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix too. \n\nTherefore, the multiplication is well defined and \n\n$$\nA\\cdot {I}_{2}=A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292861 :question/math-academy-id "q-275522" :question/problem "Calculate the product $D{I}_{2}$ given that ${I}_{2}$ is the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix and\n$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n| $D\\cdot {I}_{2}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Any matrix, when multiplied by an identity matrix, remains unchanged (assuming that the multiplication is well defined).\n\nThe matrix $D$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and ${I}_{2}$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix too. \n\nTherefore, the multiplication is well defined and \n\n$$\nD\\cdot {I}_{2}=D=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311865 :question/math-academy-id "q-337652" :question/problem "Calculate the product $Q{I}_{3}$ given that ${I}_{3}$ is the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix and\n$Q=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Q\\cdot {I}_{3}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} | {{field-6}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-7}} | {{field-8}} | {{field-9}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "Any matrix, when multiplied by an identity matrix, remains unchanged (assuming that the multiplication is well defined).\n\nThe matrix $Q$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and ${I}_{3}$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix too. \n\nTherefore, the multiplication is well defined and \n\n$$\nQ\\cdot {I}_{3}=Q=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311867 :question/math-academy-id "q-337655" :question/problem "Calculate the product ${I}_{3}B$ given that ${I}_{3}$ is the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix and\n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -6 \\\\ -5 & 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${I}_{3}\\cdot B=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} | {{field-6}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-7}} | {{field-8}} | {{field-9}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "Any matrix, when multiplied by an identity matrix, remains unchanged (assuming that the multiplication is well defined).\n\nThe matrix $B$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and ${I}_{3}$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix too. \n\nTherefore, the multiplication is well defined and \n\n$$\n{I}_{3}\\cdot B=B=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -6 \\\\ -5 & 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345087 :question/math-academy-id "q-75472" :question/problem "Calculate the product ${I}_{2}G$ given that ${I}_{2}$ is the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix and\n$G=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Any matrix, when multiplied by an identity matrix, remains unchanged (assuming that the multiplication is well defined).\n\nThe matrix ${I}_{2}$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $G$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix too. \n\nTherefore, the multiplication is well defined and \n\n$$\n{I}_{2}\\cdot G=G=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345088 :question/math-academy-id "q-75473" :question/problem "Calculate the product $J{I}_{2}$ given that ${I}_{2}$ is the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix and\n$J=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Any matrix, when multiplied by an identity matrix, remains unchanged (assuming that the multiplication is well defined).\n\nThe matrix $J$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and ${I}_{2}$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix too. \n\nTherefore, the multiplication is well defined and \n\n$$\nJ\\cdot {I}_{2}=J=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345089 :question/math-academy-id "q-75479" :question/problem "Calculate the product $Q{I}_{2}$ given that ${I}_{2}$ is the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix and\n$Q=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Any matrix, when multiplied by an identity matrix, remains unchanged (assuming that the multiplication is well defined).\n\nThe matrix $Q$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and ${I}_{2}$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix too. \n\nTherefore, the multiplication is well defined and \n\n$$\nQ\\cdot {I}_{2}=Q=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393535 :question/math-academy-id "q-75474" :question/problem "Calculate the product ${I}_{3}K$ given that ${I}_{3}$ is the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix and\n$K=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Any matrix, when multiplied by an identity matrix, remains unchanged (assuming that the multiplication is well defined).\n\nThe matrix ${I}_{3}$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $K$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix too. \n\nTherefore, the multiplication is well defined and \n\n$$\n{I}_{3}\\cdot K=K=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Matrix by the Identity Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100876 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100697 :question/math-academy-id "e-4410" :question/problem "Calculate the product $C D,$ if\n\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n\nand \n\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $D$ is the $2$nd *column* of the identity matrix $I_3.$\n\nSince we're multiplying the $3\\times 3$ matrix $C$ by the $2$nd *column* of the identity matrix $I_3,$ the result must be the $2$nd *column* of $C.$ Therefore,\n\n\n\n$$\nCD=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n\n\nDon't believe it? Let's check the long way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CD & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 0 \\\\ -1\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 1+(-4)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 5\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101407 :question/math-academy-id "q-41949" :question/problem "Find the product $AB$, if\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{7} & -3 & 3.5 \\\\ -2 & 2\\sqrt{5} & -2.5 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$\nand \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $B$ is the $1$st *column* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3}.$\n\nSince we're multiplying the $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ by the $1$st *column* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3},$ the result must be the $1$st *column* of $A.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nAB=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{7} \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the long way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{7} & -3 & 3.5 \\\\ -2 & 2\\sqrt{5} & -2.5 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{7}\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot 0+3.5\\cdot 0 \\\\ (-2)\\cdot 1+2\\sqrt{5}\\cdot 0+(-2.5)\\cdot 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 0+5\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{7} \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101408 :question/math-academy-id "q-41950" :question/problem "Find the product $AB$, if\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$B = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -\\sqrt{3} \\\\ -3 & 2\\sqrt{2} & -3.2 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265864 :question/math-academy-id "q-130247" :question/problem "Find the product $AB$, if\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $B$ is the $2$nd *column* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3}.$\n\nSince we're multiplying the $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ by the $2$nd *column* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3},$ the result must be the $2$nd *column* of $A.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nAB=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the long way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot 0+(\\frac{-\\sqrt{2}}{2})\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot 0+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 0 \\\\ 0\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292862 :question/math-academy-id "q-275527" :question/problem "Find the product $AB,$ if\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & -5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & \\sqrt{5} & -6 \\\\ 7 & -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nand \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $AB=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $B$ is the $2$nd *column* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3}.$\n\nSince we're multiplying the $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ by the $2$nd *column* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3},$ the result must be the $2$nd *column* of $A.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nAB=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the long way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & -5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & \\sqrt{5} & -6 \\\\ 7 & -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5}\\cdot 0+(-5)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0 \\\\ 3\\cdot 0+\\sqrt{5}\\cdot 1+(-6)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 7\\cdot 0+(-8)\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311868 :question/math-academy-id "q-337661" :question/problem "Find the product $AB,$ if\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 & -2\\sqrt{6} \\\\ -1 & 7 & \\sqrt{6} \\\\ 5 & 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix}$\nand \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $AB=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $B$ is the $3$rd *column* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3}.$\n\nSince we're multiplying the $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ by the $3$rd *column* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3},$ the result must be the $3$rd *column* of $A.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nAB=\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\sqrt{6} \\\\ \\sqrt{6} \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the long way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 & -2\\sqrt{6} \\\\ -1 & 7 & \\sqrt{6} \\\\ 5 & 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4\\cdot 0+(-3)\\cdot 0+(-2\\sqrt{6})\\cdot 1 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot 0+7\\cdot 0+\\sqrt{6}\\cdot 1 \\\\ 5\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 0+(-8)\\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\sqrt{6} \\\\ \\sqrt{6} \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311869 :question/math-academy-id "q-337668" :question/problem "Find the product $AB$, if\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nand \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & \\sqrt{3} & 5 \\\\ 4\\sqrt{2} & -1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & -3\\sqrt{5} & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n| $AB=$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is the $2$nd *row* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3}.$\n\nSince we're multiplying the $3\\times 3$ matrix $B$ by the $2$nd *row* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3}$ on the *left*, the result must be the $2$nd *row* of $B.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nAB=\\begin{bmatrix}4\\sqrt{2} & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the long way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & \\sqrt{3} & 5 \\\\ 4\\sqrt{2} & -1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & -3\\sqrt{5} & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4\\sqrt{2} & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311870 :question/math-academy-id "q-337673" :question/problem "Find the product $EF$, if\n$E=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand \n$F=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} & -8 & \\sqrt{5} & 14 \\\\ -2 & \\frac{7}{5} & -\\frac{\\pi }{2} & 9 \\\\ \\sqrt{17} & -6 & 0 & \\frac{e}{3} \\\\ -11 & \\sqrt{19} & -4 & -\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n| $EF=$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $E$ is the $4$th *row* of the identity matrix ${I}_{4}.$\n\nSince we're multiplying the $4\\times 4$ matrix $F$ by the $4$th *row* of the identity matrix ${I}_{4}$ on the *left*, the result must be the $4$th *row* of $F.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nEF=\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & \\sqrt{19} & -4 & -\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the long way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}EF & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} & -8 & \\sqrt{5} & 14 \\\\ -2 & \\frac{7}{5} & -\\frac{\\pi }{2} & 9 \\\\ \\sqrt{17} & -6 & 0 & \\frac{e}{3} \\\\ -11 & \\sqrt{19} & -4 & -\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & \\sqrt{19} & -4 & -\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326916 :question/math-academy-id "q-41944" :question/problem "Find the product $AB$, if\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nand \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & 4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\sqrt{2} & -3.2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is the $2$nd *row* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3}.$\n\nSince we're multiplying the $3\\times 3$ matrix $B$ by the $2$nd *row* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3}$ on the *left*, the result must be the $2$nd *row* of $B.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nAB=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2\\sqrt{2} & -3.2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the long way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & 4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\sqrt{2} & -3.2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2\\sqrt{2} & -3.2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326918 :question/math-academy-id "q-41977" :question/problem "Find the product $AB$, if\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nand \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & -\\sqrt{7} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{5} & -3.2 \\\\ \\sqrt{6} & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is the $2$nd *row* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3}.$\n\nSince we're multiplying the $3\\times 3$ matrix $B$ by the $2$nd *row* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3}$ on the *left*, the result must be the $2$nd *row* of $B.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nAB=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{5} & -3.2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the long way: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & -\\sqrt{7} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{5} & -3.2 \\\\ \\sqrt{6} & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{5} & -3.2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326919 :question/math-academy-id "q-41979" :question/problem "Find the product $AB$, if\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nand \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}4\\sqrt{2} & 1.5 & 2 \\\\ -3.5 & -2\\sqrt{3} & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -\\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is the $2$nd *row* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3}.$\n\nSince we're multiplying the $3\\times 3$ matrix $B$ by the $2$nd *row* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3}$ on the *left*, the result must be the $2$nd *row* of $B.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nAB=\\begin{bmatrix}-3.5 & -2\\sqrt{3} & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the long way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4\\sqrt{2} & 1.5 & 2 \\\\ -3.5 & -2\\sqrt{3} & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -\\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3.5 & -2\\sqrt{3} & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392435 :question/math-academy-id "q-41943" :question/problem "Find the product $AB$, if\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & -5 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 3\\sqrt{2} & -3.5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -4\\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $B$ is the $3$rd *column* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3}.$\n\nSince we're multiplying the $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ by the $3$rd *column* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3}$, the result must be the $3$rd column of $A.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nAB=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3.5 \\\\ -4\\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's check the long way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & -5 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 3\\sqrt{2} & -3.5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -4\\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 0+(-5)\\cdot 0+5\\cdot 1 \\\\ (-5)\\cdot 0+3\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 0+(-3.5)\\cdot 1 \\\\ 1\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 0+(-4\\sqrt{5})\\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3.5 \\\\ -4\\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546081 :question/problem "Find the product $AB,$ if\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{13} & -3 & 6 \\\\ 3 & \\sqrt{3} & -1 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nand \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $AB=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $B$ is the $2$nd *column* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3}.$\n\nSince we're multiplying the $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ by the $2$nd *column* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3},$ the result must be the $2$nd *column* of $A.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nAB=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the long way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{13} & -3 & 6 \\\\ 3 & \\sqrt{3} & -1 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{13}\\cdot 0+(-3)\\cdot 1+6\\cdot 0 \\\\ 3\\cdot 0+\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 7\\cdot 0+(-1)\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546083 :question/problem "Find the product $AB$, if\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{10} & -1 & 4.6 \\\\ -4 & 2\\sqrt{11} & -1.7 \\\\ 3\\sqrt{6} & 6 & 9\\end{bmatrix}$\nand \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $B$ is the $3$rd *column* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3}.$\n\nSince we're multiplying the $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ by the $3$rd *column* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3},$ the result must be the $3$rd *column* of $A.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nAB=\\begin{bmatrix}4.6 \\\\ -1.7 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the long way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{10} & -1 & 4.6 \\\\ -4 & 2\\sqrt{11} & -1.7 \\\\ 3\\sqrt{6} & 6 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{10}\\cdot 0+(-1)\\cdot 0+4.6\\cdot 1 \\\\ (-4)\\cdot 0+2\\sqrt{11}\\cdot 0+(-1.7)\\cdot 1 \\\\ 3\\sqrt{6}\\cdot 0+6\\cdot 0+9\\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4.6 \\\\ -1.7 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Matrix by a Column or Row of the Identity Matrix"}] :topic/title "Multiplying a Matrix by the Identity Matrix"}
